@startuml observability
title Observability - three questions, three paths, one traceId

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center

actor "operator" as OP

node "individuals-api" as API {
  component "AuthMetrics\nsix counters, two timers" as METERS
  component "RequestLogFilter\nJSON to stdout" as LOGS
  component "OpenTelemetry\nspans" as SPANS
}

node "prometheus" as PROM
node "alloy" as ALLOY
database "loki" as LOKI
database "tempo" as TEMPO
node "grafana" as GRAF

' --- metrics: pulled ---
PROM -[#0b6e4f,bold]-> METERS : **scrape** every 15s\nGET /actuator/prometheus

' --- logs: written, then collected ---
LOGS -[#b45309,bold]-> ALLOY : stdout\nread through the Docker socket
ALLOY -[#b45309,bold]-> LOKI : push

' --- traces: pushed by the application itself ---
SPANS -[#6d28d9,bold]-> TEMPO : **push** OTLP\ntempo:4318/v1/traces

GRAF --> PROM
GRAF --> LOKI
GRAF --> TEMPO
OP --> GRAF

note right of PROM
  **How many, and how fast?**

  Numbers over time. The application
  keeps no history - it only knows
  "right now it is 4". Prometheus
  walks over and writes it down.

  Ask **rate(...)**, never the raw
  value: a counter resets to zero on
  restart, and rate knows that.
end note

note right of ALLOY
  **What actually happened?**

  The app writes JSON to stdout and
  nothing more. Docker keeps that in a
  file that dies with the container, so
  an agent has to read it and ship it.

  Alloy turns Docker metadata into Loki
  labels - service, container, env - and
  lifts level, traceId and spanId out of
  the JSON body.
end note

note right of TEMPO
  **Where did the request go, and
  where did it get stuck?**

  One traceId per request, one spanId
  per operation inside it. A parent id
  on each span is what builds the tree.

  This is the only place that shows one
  login costing seven calls outwards.
end note

note bottom of GRAF
  **Prometheus pulls. The application pushes.**
  That is why the app's address lives in prometheus.yml
  while Tempo's address lives in application.yml.
  One arrow points each way, and mixing them up is
  the usual reason a panel stays empty.
end note

note bottom of LOKI
  **traceId ties a log line to its trace.**
  It is in both, so one click in Grafana goes from
  a line to the whole request.

  Metrics carry no traceId and cannot: a counter is
  "how many in total", with no individual requests
  inside it. Metrics say **that** something is wrong;
  logs and traces say **what**.
end note

@enduml
